    "knowledge_point": "Calculating Joint Probabilities",
    "problem": "The partially complete joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.03$ | $0.07$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=4$ | $∗$ | $∗$ | $0.9$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $?$ | $∗$ | $1$ |\n\nWhat is the value of $P(Y=-1)?$",
    "worked_solution": "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our random variables are independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,-1) & ={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-1) \\\\ P(X=2,Y=-1) & =P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=-1) \\\\ 0.03 & =0.1\\cdot P(Y=-1) \\\\ P(Y=-1) & =\\frac{0.03}{0.1} \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
